A||N||8||Will||Rod end swaybar end links (On the Cheap!)||||1||
Z||000000||Will||11-18-2000||09:35 PM||||You handling guys will like this one:<P>Your will need four rod ends ~1/2" shank diameter (threaded). YOu can get these from McMaster-Carr (www.mcmaster.com).<BR>Remove standard swaybar end links, and stick the threaded shanks of the rod ends through the resulting empty holes in the control arm and swaybar. The heads of the rod ends should be toward eachother; i.e. in the front, the shanks should be inserted up through the swaybar and down through the control arm.<BR>Tighten a nut of appropriate size down on the back of the rod end to hold it firmly in place; the nut will sandwich the swaybar/control arm between itself and the head of the rod end. Washers or a second nut on the head side of the swaybar/control arm may be needed.<BR>To link the two together is not hard. One of the simplest ways is to take two strips of steel, each with a hole close to each end. Put one strip on each side of one rod end and put a bolt through the whole shebang, then attach similarly to the other rod end. Ingenuitive folks could figure out that they can use channel instead of strips, or take a bite out of each end of a piece of square tubing, or put threaded shackels on each end of a piece of threaded rod to have a setup with adjustable preload, etc.||||reg||1
Z||000001||mshill||11-19-2000||12:28 AM||||You know... four years of college and a degree in Math/Computer Science and I still need pictures.  I can't make heads nor tails out of what you just described here.<P>Any diagrams/pictures?||||reg||1
