Z||000003||Will||11-19-2000||01:40 AM||||+++++++++ __<BR>++++++___|__||__|__|__|_    &lt;- Nut<BR>__________|__|____|   &lt;- End of Swaybar<BR>++++++++/++++\<BR>+++++++/++__++\<BR>++++++(++(__)++)     &lt;- Head of Rod-End (pulled down tightly against swaybar by nut)<BR>+++++++\++++++/<BR>++++++++\____/<P><BR>+++++++++____<BR>++++++++/++++\<BR>+++++++/++__++\<BR>++++++(++(__)++)     &lt;- Head of Rod-End (pulled down tightly against control arm by nut)<BR>+++++++\+++++ /<BR>_________\++++/___________<BR>__________|++|_____________     &lt;- Control arm<BR>++++++|__|__|__|               &lt;- Nut<BR>+++++++++|__|                  &lt;- End of rod-end shank <P>Connect the two rod ends with steel straps that look like this:<BR>  ++__<BR>  +/++\<BR>  |+()+|<BR>  |++++|<BR>  |++++|<BR>  |++++|<BR>  |++++|<BR>  |++++|<BR>  |++++|<BR>  |++++|<BR>  |++++|<BR>  |++++|<BR>  |++++|<BR>  |++++|<BR>  |++++|<BR>  |+()+|<BR>  +\__/<P>Can you make heads or tales of that?<BR>I put +'s where the spaces should be<P><BR>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 11-19-2000).]<P>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 11-19-2000).]<P>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 11-19-2000).]<P>Afte four and a half (working on five) years of college for Physics and Math, one would think I should be able to draw. Not so.<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 11-19-2000).]||204.111.49.35||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||11-19-2000||01:23 PM||||That make some sense now. You're useing spherical rod ends to replace the bushing set. <P>You'll want to find nylon lined spheres for that. They will take the vibration and bumps better.<P>You also want to watch the orientation of the eye's to make sure you don't bind them up. There's only so much movment allowed in certain directions.<P>You also want the strap pretty heavy. remember that when one end is pulling the other is compressing.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ironhorse||11-19-2000||01:26 PM||||MAKES NOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOO SENSE TO ME as of what you're describing or try to describe.   Exactly what is the purpose of doing this?  I would not hook up tie rod end to swaybar for any reason or purpose.  Tierod end has very little play in it and that's the last thing that swaybar needs.  I don't think the engineer screw up with the design of sway bar.  It's perfect in the way it was design to be use and mounted.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||StuGood||11-19-2000||01:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ironhorse:<BR><B>...I don't think the engineer screw up with the design of sway bar.  It's perfect in the way it was design to be use and mounted.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, thank you.  Yore write - us engineers <B>never</B> screw up.  Well, okay - occasional typoze... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>If you think the design is perfect (for you)the way you found it, great  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> - leave it the way it is.  But, if you would like to try increasing the effective stiffness of your existing stabilizer bar(s), the above idea is one way to do it.<P>Beauty of it is, it's very reversible - if you try it and don't like it (<B>too</B> much roll stiffness), you can always unbolt everything, put it back and try something less drastic (polyurethane bushings, etc.).  And it uses standard pieces one might have lying around.  I like the idea, myself, especially if you find some greasable end links.  Pretty slick (yes - a little grease joke slipped in there, sorry  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">).<P>But I probably won't be doing this.  Adding roll stiffness (especially if you do to one end of the car) can affect the handling in ways you need to be ready for (insert the standard warnings/disclaimers here).  <P>Like the shampoo bottle says - Modify, test drive, repeat...   ||||reg||6
Z||000007||Will||11-20-2000||11:54 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ironhorse:<BR><B>MAKES NOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOO SENSE TO ME as of what you're describing or try to describe.   Exactly what is the purpose of doing this?  I would not hook up tie rod end to swaybar for any reason or purpose.  Tierod end has very little play in it and that's the last thing that swaybar needs.  I don't think the engineer screw up with the design of sway bar.  It's perfect in the way it was design to be use and mounted.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>     I have to take issue with this. Why do high performance sports cars such as the Corvette use rod-end swaybar links from the factory if rubber bushings are ideal?<BR>     The truth is that rubber bushings are cheaper and easier to fabricate than rod ends. On a low-end car such as the Fiero, rod-ends were not worth the additional manufacturing expense.<BR>     They will improve the performance of an anti-sway bar. They can even be used as a tuning tool: using rod ends up front with rubber in the rear is analagous to using a slightly stiffer front bar.<BR>     But if you don't want superior performance, that's your call.||||reg||1
Z||000008||lowCG||11-20-2000||02:37 PM||||Thanks for the tip!  I already have the rod ends on the rear,they're really the only way to go if you're serious about handling,as there's no bind like with rubber,or even worse,polyurethane end links.The front could really do with a little loosening up,now it understeers slightly going into corners,and this might be the ticket,if not it'll at least ride better.Thanks||||reg||1
